{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":273040,"duration_api_ms":198090,"ttft_ms":1610,"ttft_stream_ms":1609,"time_to_request_ms":249,"num_turns":27,"result":"Iteration complete. One experiment, green build, one new logged run.\n\n## Summary\n\n**exp003 — extract→coverage-audit (2-call pipeline)** — DEV quality **0.931**, **DISCARDED** (−0.024 vs exp001's 0.955).\n\n**Hypothesis:** exp001's entire residual loss was pure *recall* on three multi-task cases (dropped buried/prerequisite tasks), with precision a perfect 1.0. I added a second scoped call that sees the committed task list and adds *only* genuinely-missing stated tasks — skipped entirely when the base list is empty (protecting the solved zero-task gate), with a deterministic dedup safety net.\n\n**Result — honest read:** The recall lever genuinely works on the hardest interleaved inputs (`multi_errands` 0.8→1.0; `interleaved_deck` recovered its Q3-numbers prerequisite → judged a tie; recall rose 0.924→0.985). But it **over-fired on single-task cases**, re-emitting a paraphrased/deadline-qualified variant of the lone task even with the list visible (`dedup_groceries` and `mixed_weekend` each gained a duplicate; precision 1.0→0.909). String dedup can't catch paraphrase duplicates. This is the same precision↔recall tension that sank exp002 — the recall gain on hard cases is exactly cancelled by precision loss on easy ones.\n\n**exp001 remains best.** Logged a clear next lever: gate the audit to multi-task contexts only, or tighten its add-criterion to require a verb/object absent from every listed item.","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"bad1bc8b-f363-46d8-a022-012377280d35","total_cost_usd":0.7888012499999999,"usage":{"input_tokens":52,"cache_creation_input_tokens":31919,"cache_read_input_tokens":553527,"output_tokens":12469,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input